;; A control of the guess of liftings of the test tool (lifting_guess in
;; ../../tools/flsemantics.ml), on a pair of texts written by hand: the term
;; after is read from a text (the option -after), and Simplify is not run.
;; `dune promote` updates the expected file.
;;
;; box_in_slot_kept_by_let: the third test that the guess makes of a boxed
;; number that a closure holds in a value slot, "of which the term after has no
;; Let". No unit made from OCaml is known whose term after has one. Where the
;; Simplify of this tree simplifies a Let that boxes a number whose contents
;; are a known constant, and the variable of the Let has the normal name mode,
;; it puts in the place of the boxed number a symbol that is bound to it
;; (simplify_named.ml; reify, in middle_end/flambda2/types/reify.ml, asks for
;; the lifting whatever the allocation mode). The Lets that Simplify makes
;; itself have not been gone through. Here the term before boxes the constant
;; 1000 once; a closure that nothing uses holds the boxed number in a value
;; slot; and the fields of the module are the boxed number and a block of two
;; constants. The term after binds a symbol to the block, keeps the boxed
;; number by a Let, and has no closure. The guess gives the block that symbol
;; and, as the term after boxes the same constant by a Let, gives the boxed
;; number none: the pair is validated at the first asking, and the tool prints
;; of it what it printed before the guess had this case.
;;
;; With the third test taken out (measured with a build that is not in the
;; tree), the output has one line more, "(at the second asking, ...)": the
;; first asking refuses the pair and the second validates it. The reading of
;; that, from the source of the tool and not printed by it: the guess gives
;; the boxed number a symbol that neither term has, so that at the first
;; asking a field of the module is that symbol in one term and the variable
;; of the Let in the other; the guess of the second asking is made from the
;; term before normalised, where the closure is gone and nothing holds the
;; boxed number in a value slot. So the test decides at which asking this
;; pair is validated.
;;
;; No pair is known that is validated with the test and not validated without
;; it. What was tried, with -after, for this control: two pairs whose term
;; after keeps the closure (one with its value slot; one as the compiler's
;; Simplify leaves the closure of ../ocaml/boxed_number_in_slot, with none)
;; were not validated, with the test or without it (a closure of the term
;; after is the same as none of the term before: ../guard/dune,
;; after_same_text); and two pairs whose closure nothing uses, and of which
;; nothing else needs a lifting, were validated with nothing lifted, with the
;; test and without it. That is of those trials and of no others.

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  box_in_slot_kept_by_let.output
  (run
   ../../tools/flsemantics.exe
   -after
   %{dep:box_in_slot_kept_by_let_after.fl}
   %{dep:box_in_slot_kept_by_let.fl})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff box_in_slot_kept_by_let.expected box_in_slot_kept_by_let.output)))
